<GuideLayout
  title="LittleFS Audio - MAX98357A I2S"
  description="Store and play audio clips from LittleFS on an ESP32 with a MAX98357A I2S amplifier. No recompile to change clips - upload WAV files directly to the filesystem."
  toc={[
    {href: '#what-you-need', label: 'What You Need'},
    {href: '#how-it-works', label: 'How It Works'},
    {href: '#wiring', label: 'Wiring'},
    {href: '#before-you-start', label: 'Before You Start'},
    {href: '#preparing-clips', label: 'Preparing Your Clips'},
    {href: '#uploading', label: 'Uploading to LittleFS'},
    {href: '#sketch', label: 'The Sketch'},
    {href: '#troubleshooting', label: 'Troubleshooting'},
  ]}
>

  <div class="hero">
    <div class="category">Audio &bull; ESP32</div>
    <h1>LittleFS Audio<br>MAX98357A I2S</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Storage:</strong> LittleFS filesystem - max out your chip's full memory potential</div>
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Board:</strong> ESP32 Dev Board (38-pin) or ESP32-C3</div>
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Amp:</strong> MAX98357A</div>
    </div>
    <div class="series-step">MAX98357A I2S Series - Step 4 of 5 - LittleFS audio</div>
    <div class="badge badge-wip">&#9881; Under Construction</div>
  </div>

  <div class="terminal-note">
    This guide continues from <a href="/guides/max98357a-multiple-clips" class="term-link">Step 3 - Multiple Clips &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <!-- What You Need -->
  <div class="section" id="what-you-need">
    <h2>What You Need</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Parts</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/glossary/esp32-i2s" class="term-link">ESP32 Dev Board (38-pin)</a> or <a href="/glossary/esp32c3-i2s" class="term-link">ESP32-C3</a></li>
          <li><a href="/glossary/max98357a" class="term-link">MAX98357A I2S amplifier module</a></li>
          <li><a href="/glossary/speaker-i2s" class="term-link">4-8&#8486; speaker (1W or above)</a></li>
          <li>Jumper wires</li>
          <li>USB-C cable for flashing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Software</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Arduino IDE with ESP32 board support</li>
          <li>Arduino ESP32 LittleFS upload plugin</li>
          <li><code>ffmpeg</code> - convert audio files</li>
          <li>Python 3</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- How It Works -->
  <div class="section" id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <p>In Steps 2 and 3, audio clips are compiled directly into the sketch as C arrays in PROGMEM. That approach is simple but every clip change requires a recompile and reflash of the full sketch - and the number of clips is limited by the app partition size.</p>
    <p>LittleFS is a small filesystem that lives in its own partition on the ESP32's flash. Audio files sit in the filesystem as plain WAV files. The sketch reads them at runtime and streams them to I2S. To change or add clips, you upload a new filesystem image - no recompile needed.</p>
    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>Partition scheme matters.</strong>
      For LittleFS audio, use <strong>No OTA (2MB APP / 2MB SPIFFS)</strong> - it gives the best balance of sketch space and audio file space. Set it in Arduino IDE under <strong>Tools &rarr; Partition Scheme</strong> before flashing. <a href="/glossary/partition-scheme" class="term-link">Full partition scheme guide &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Wiring -->
  <div class="section" id="wiring">
    <h2>Wiring</h2>
    <p>Wiring is identical to Step 2 - no changes needed.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>MAX98357A Pin</th><th>ESP32 Dev Board</th><th>ESP32-C3</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>VIN</td><td>5V</td><td>5V</td><td>5V recommended - 3.3V works at lower volume</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GND</td><td>GND</td><td>GND</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>LRC</td><td>GPIO14</td><td>GPIO5</td><td>I2S word select (LRCLK)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>BCLK</td><td>GPIO27</td><td>GPIO4</td><td>I2S bit clock</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DIN</td><td>GPIO13</td><td>GPIO6</td><td>I2S data out</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SD</td><td>Not connected</td><td>Not connected</td><td>Leave floating - amp enabled</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GAIN</td><td>Not connected</td><td>Not connected</td><td>Leave floating - 9dB gain</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Before You Start -->
  <div class="section" id="before-you-start">
    <h2>Before You Start</h2>
    <p>These guides are ordered by complexity but each one stands alone - if you believe and have courage, stand tall and boldly continue. If this is your first time, start at <a href="/guides/max98357a-wiring-tone-test" class="term-link">Step 1 &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Preparing Clips -->
  <div class="section" id="preparing-clips">
    <h2>Preparing Your Clips</h2>
    <p>Step 3 needed a Python script to convert WAV samples into a C array compiled into the sketch. LittleFS is different - the sketch reads WAV files directly from the filesystem at runtime, so the WAV file is the final product. ffmpeg just makes sure it is the right format and the sketch streams it byte by byte. No Python, no header files.</p>
    <p>The pattern is the same as Step 3: create a staging folder, cut your clips into it one by one, then batch convert them all into the <code>data/</code> folder.</p>

    <h3>Create the data folder</h3>
    <p>Inside your sketch folder, create a folder called exactly <code>data</code>. This is where LittleFS picks up the files to upload.</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>mkdir ~/Arduino/YourSketchName/data</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p>Replace <code>YourSketchName</code> with your actual sketch folder name. Or do it in your file manager - open the sketch folder, right-click, and create a new folder named <code>data</code>.</p>

    <h3>Create a clips folder</h3>
    <p>Create a staging folder to collect your raw clips before converting:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>mkdir ~/myclips</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>

    <h3>Find your clip in a video</h3>
    <p>Open the source video or audio in VLC or a player of your choice. Note the timestamp for where your clip starts and ends, then cut it into the staging folder:</p>
    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      <strong>Reading the timestamp:</strong>
      In VLC the current position shows in the bottom left as HH:MM:SS. Pause at the start of your clip, write down the time, then do the same for the end.
    </div>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>ffmpeg -i ~/Videos/myvideo.mp4 -ss 00:01:23 -to 00:01:27 ~/myclips/clip1.wav</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">If the source file is in your home folder:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>ffmpeg -i ~/mysound.wav -ss 00:01:23 -to 00:01:27 ~/myclips/clip1.wav</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">If the source file is in Downloads:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>ffmpeg -i ~/Downloads/mysound.mp3 -ss 00:01:23 -to 00:01:27 ~/myclips/clip1.wav</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">If the source is a video - ffmpeg extracts the audio automatically:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>ffmpeg -i ~/Videos/myvideo.mp4 -ss 00:01:23 -to 00:01:27 ~/myclips/clip1.wav</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">If the filename has spaces - wrap it in quotes:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>ffmpeg -i "/home/j/My Audio File.wav" -ss 00:01:23 -to 00:01:27 ~/myclips/clip1.wav</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p>Repeat for each clip, changing the timestamps and filename each time. Once all clips are in the folder, move on.</p>

    <h3>Convert all clips</h3>
    <p>Once all your clips are in <code>~/myclips/</code>, run this once to convert the whole batch. The converted files land next to the originals in <code>~/myclips/</code> with <code>_i2s</code> added to the name so they don't overwrite them:</p>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`for f in ~/myclips/*.wav; do
  name=$(basename "$f" .wav)
  ffmpeg -y -i "$f" -ar 44100 -ac 1 -acodec pcm_s16le ~/myclips/\${name}_i2s.wav
done`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p>You will now have both the originals (<code>clip1.wav</code>) and the converted versions (<code>clip1_i2s.wav</code>) side by side. Move the <code>_i2s.wav</code> files into your <code>data/</code> folder - those are the ones the sketch needs.</p>
    <p>Unlike Step 3, LittleFS filenames do not become C variable names so there are no strict rules - <code>my-clip.wav</code> or <code>hello world.wav</code> will work. That said, sticking to letters, numbers and underscores is good practice and avoids surprises. The sketch plays files in filesystem order, so numbering them (<code>clip1.wav</code>, <code>clip2.wav</code>) gives you predictable playback.</p>
    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>Why 44100 Hz?</strong>
      It is the CD standard - the lowest sample rate that captures the full range of human hearing cleanly. Drop to 22050 Hz for speech to halve the file size with no noticeable quality loss. Change <code>-ar</code> here and <code>SAMPLE_RATE</code> in the sketch to match.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Uploading -->
  <div class="section" id="uploading">
    <h2>Uploading to LittleFS</h2>
    <p>Arduino IDE 2.x needs the LittleFS upload plugin installed before you can upload the data folder.</p>

    <h3>Install the plugin</h3>
    <p>The plugin adds the upload option to Arduino IDE. <a href="/glossary/littlefs-plugin" class="term-link">Full install instructions - IDE 2.x and 1.x &rarr;</a></p>

    <h3>Upload the filesystem</h3>
    <p>With your sketch open and your ESP32 connected:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Close the Serial Monitor if it is open - it locks the port</li>
      <li>In Arduino IDE 2.x, open the command palette: <strong>Ctrl+Shift+P</strong></li>
      <li>Type <strong>Upload LittleFS</strong> and select it</li>
      <li>Wait for the upload to complete - you will see <strong>LittleFS upload finished</strong> in the output</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>Flash the sketch first, then upload the filesystem.</strong>
      The filesystem upload does not touch the app partition. Flash the sketch once via USB, then upload the filesystem. After that, updating clips only requires a new filesystem upload - no sketch reflash needed.
    </div>

    <h3>Confirm the upload worked</h3>
    <p>Flash this diagnostic sketch, open Serial Monitor at <strong>115200 baud</strong>, and it will list every file in LittleFS with its name, size, and total space used. If it shows empty, the filesystem upload did not land.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`#include <LittleFS.h>

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(1000);

  if (!LittleFS.begin(true)) {
    Serial.println("LittleFS mount failed");
    return;
  }

  Serial.println("\\n--- LittleFS contents ---");
  File root = LittleFS.open("/");
  File file = root.openNextFile();
  int count = 0;

  while (file) {
    Serial.print(file.name());
    Serial.print("  ");
    Serial.print(file.size());
    Serial.println(" bytes");
    count++;
    file = root.openNextFile();
  }

  if (count == 0) Serial.println("(empty - no files found)");
  Serial.print("--- ");
  Serial.print(count);
  Serial.println(" file(s) ---");

  Serial.print("Total: ");
  Serial.print(LittleFS.totalBytes() / 1024);
  Serial.print("KB  Used: ");
  Serial.print(LittleFS.usedBytes() / 1024);
  Serial.println("KB");
}

void loop() {}`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Sketch -->
  <div class="section" id="sketch">
    <h2>The Sketch</h2>
    <p>Reads every <code>.wav</code> file from LittleFS in order and plays them in a continuous loop. Add or remove clips by uploading a new filesystem image - the sketch never needs to change.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`/*
 * We stand on the shoulders of giants when we build
 * with knowledge gained from others' efforts.
 * That doesn't make us giants. Be humble.
 * Create with care. Open source is the way.
 *
 * MAX98357A I2S - LittleFS Audio
 * --------------------------------
 * Reads WAV files from LittleFS and plays them
 * via I2S in a continuous loop. 44100Hz 16-bit mono.
 *
 * Board:   ESP32 Dev Board (38-pin) or ESP32-C3
 * Amp:     MAX98357A
 *
 * Wiring (ESP32 Dev Board):
 *   BCLK -> GPIO27  LRC -> GPIO14  DIN -> GPIO13
 *
 * Wiring (ESP32-C3):
 *   BCLK -> GPIO4   LRC -> GPIO5   DIN -> GPIO6
 *
 * Partition scheme: No OTA (2MB APP / 2MB SPIFFS)
 *
 * Open source - MIT Licence
 * Electronic Zoology - field notes from the garage
 * https://electroniczoology.com/guides/max98357a-littlefs
 */

#include <driver/i2s.h>
#include <LittleFS.h>

// ESP32 Dev Board (38-pin)
#define I2S_BCLK    27
#define I2S_LRCLK   14
#define I2S_DOUT    13
// ESP32-C3 - comment out the three lines above and uncomment these:
//#define I2S_BCLK    4
//#define I2S_LRCLK   5
//#define I2S_DOUT    6

#define SAMPLE_RATE  44100
#define VOLUME       0.8f
#define BUF_SAMPLES  256
#define WAV_HEADER   44   // standard PCM WAV header size in bytes

int16_t i2s_buf[BUF_SAMPLES * 2];

void playFile(const char* path) {
  File f = LittleFS.open(path, "r");
  if (!f) return;

  f.seek(WAV_HEADER);  // skip WAV header, jump straight to PCM data

  int16_t raw[BUF_SAMPLES];
  size_t written;

  while (f.available() >= (int)(BUF_SAMPLES * 2)) {
    f.read((uint8_t*)raw, BUF_SAMPLES * 2);
    for (int i = 0; i < BUF_SAMPLES; i++) {
      int16_t s = (int16_t)(raw[i] * VOLUME);
      i2s_buf[i * 2]     = s;
      i2s_buf[i * 2 + 1] = s;
    }
    i2s_write(I2S_NUM_0, i2s_buf, BUF_SAMPLES * 4, &written, portMAX_DELAY);
  }

  f.close();
}

void setup() {
  LittleFS.begin();

  i2s_config_t cfg = {
    .mode                 = (i2s_mode_t)(I2S_MODE_MASTER | I2S_MODE_TX),
    .sample_rate          = SAMPLE_RATE,
    .bits_per_sample      = I2S_BITS_PER_SAMPLE_16BIT,
    .channel_format       = I2S_CHANNEL_FMT_RIGHT_LEFT,
    .communication_format = I2S_COMM_FORMAT_STAND_I2S,
    .intr_alloc_flags     = ESP_INTR_FLAG_LEVEL1,
    .dma_buf_count        = 8,
    .dma_buf_len          = 64,
    .use_apll             = false,
    .tx_desc_auto_clear   = true,
  };
  i2s_pin_config_t pins = {
    .bck_io_num   = I2S_BCLK,
    .ws_io_num    = I2S_LRCLK,
    .data_out_num = I2S_DOUT,
    .data_in_num  = I2S_PIN_NO_CHANGE,
  };
  i2s_driver_install(I2S_NUM_0, &cfg, 0, NULL);
  i2s_set_pin(I2S_NUM_0, &pins);
}

void loop() {
  File root = LittleFS.open("/");
  File file = root.openNextFile();
  while (file) {
    String name = file.name();
    if (!file.isDirectory() && name.endsWith(".wav")) {
      String path = "/" + name;
      playFile(path.c_str());
      delay(500);
    }
    file = root.openNextFile();
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Troubleshooting -->
  <div class="section" id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <div class="trouble-item">
      <h3>No sound - sketch runs but nothing plays</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The filesystem may not have been uploaded - confirm you ran the LittleFS upload step, not just the sketch upload</li>
        <li>Check the <code>data/</code> folder exists in your sketch folder and contains <code>.wav</code> files</li>
        <li>Serial Monitor at 115200 baud - add <code>Serial.begin(115200);</code> and <code>Serial.println(file.name());</code> inside the loop to confirm files are being found</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="trouble-item">
      <h3>LittleFS.begin() fails</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The filesystem partition has not been formatted - pass <code>true</code> to format on first use: <code>LittleFS.begin(true)</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="trouble-item">
      <h3>Distorted or wrong speed audio</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The <code>SAMPLE_RATE</code> in the sketch must match the <code>-ar</code> value used in ffmpeg - both should be <code>44100</code></li>
        <li>Confirm the WAV was converted with <code>-acodec pcm_s16le</code> - other formats produce noise or silence</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="trouble-item">
      <h3>Upload plugin not showing in Arduino IDE</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Restart Arduino IDE after installing the <code>.vsix</code> plugin</li>
        <li>Confirm the plugin was installed for the correct IDE installation if you have multiple</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

